{
  "id": "leetcode-188",
  "version": 1,
  "status": "approved",
  "title": "Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock IV",
  "summary": "Given an integer array prices where prices[i] represents the price of a stock on the ith day, and an integer k, find the maximum profit you can achieve by completing at most k transactions.",
  "statement": "Given an integer array prices where prices[i] represents the price of a stock on the ith day, and an integer k, find the maximum profit you can achieve by completing at most k transactions. You are not allowed to engage in multiple transactions simultaneously, meaning you must sell the stock before you can buy again.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 ≤ k ≤ 100; prices has 1–1000 daily prices, each from 0 to 1000.",
    "Make at most k buy-then-sell transactions, holding at most one share at a time. Transactions need not be consecutive, and doing nothing is allowed. There is no fee or cooldown."
  ],
  "topics": [
    "arrays",
    "dynamic-programming"
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
    "dynamic-programming"
  ],
  "signature": {
    "name": "maximum_trade_profit",
    "parameters": [
      {
        "name": "k",
        "annotation": "int",
        "adapter": "raw",
        "source_parameter": null
      },
      {
        "name": "prices",
        "annotation": "list[int]",
        "adapter": "raw",
        "source_parameter": null
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int",
    "return_adapter": "raw",
    "node_policy": "values",
    "result_mode": "return",
    "mutated_parameter": null,
    "immutable_parameters": []
  },
  "starter_code": "def maximum_trade_profit(k: int, prices: list[int]) -> int:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n",
  "reference_solution": "def maximum_trade_profit(k, prices):\n    if k >= len(prices) // 2:\n        return sum(max(0, prices[i] - prices[i - 1]) for i in range(1, len(prices)))\n    cash = [0] * (k + 1)\n    hold = [float(\"-inf\")] * (k + 1)\n    for price in prices:\n        for limit in range(k, 0, -1):\n            cash[limit] = max(cash[limit], hold[limit] + price)\n            hold[limit] = max(hold[limit], cash[limit - 1] - price)\n    return cash[k]\n",
  "brute_force_solution": null,
  "known_wrong_solutions": [
    "def maximum_trade_profit(k, prices):\n    return sum(max(0, b-a) for a, b in zip(prices, prices[1:]))\n",
    "def maximum_trade_profit(k, prices):\n    k = min(k, 2)\n    if k >= len(prices) // 2:\n        return sum(max(0, prices[i] - prices[i - 1]) for i in range(1, len(prices)))\n    cash = [0] * (k + 1)\n    hold = [float(\"-inf\")] * (k + 1)\n    for price in prices:\n        for limit in range(k, 0, -1):\n            cash[limit] = max(cash[limit], hold[limit] + price)\n            hold[limit] = max(hold[limit], cash[limit - 1] - price)\n    return cash[k]\n"
  ],
  "target_time_complexity": "O(n × min(k,n)); O(n) when the transaction cap is nonbinding",
  "target_space_complexity": "O(k) for bounded trading; O(1) for the unlimited shortcut",
  "estimated_minutes": 50,
  "rubric": {
    "algorithmic_insight": 3,
    "prerequisites": 2,
    "implementation": 1,
    "performance_pressure": 1,
    "edge_cases": 1
  },
  "hints": [
    "The best action depends on both whether a share is held and the transaction budget remaining.",
    "Maintain cash and holding states for each allowed transaction count.",
    "For each price and budget j: cash[j]=max(old_cash[j],old_hold[j]+price); hold[j]=max(old_hold[j],old_cash[j-1]-price). Return cash[k].",
    "Update budgets downward to retain previous-day dependencies. If k≥floor(n/2), the cap cannot restrict profit and every positive daily rise can be taken."
  ],
  "editorial": "cash[j] is the best realized profit with no stock and at most j buys; hold[j] is the best balance while holding with at most j buys. Selling preserves the budget, while buying uses one more than the previous cash state. Downward budget updates read the previous day's smaller-budget cash. With enough budget, sum positive adjacent rises instead. The independent oracle enumerates all even-length choices of transaction days and pairs them as buy/sell actions, selecting the best legal profit. Excluding same-day sell/buy pairs in this enumeration loses no optimum because such pairs can be merged without changing profit. The configurable budget is a distinct contract from the existing one- and two-trade exercises; tests require more than two trades as well as restrictive one-trade behavior.",
